===Etymology===
{{compound|en|hard|wood}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈhɑɹdˌwʊd/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-hardwood.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|mostly in botany and forestry}} The [[wood]] from any [[dicotyledon]]ous tree, without regard to its [[hardness]].
#: {{ux|en|Balsa is a '''hardwood''', but a soft hardwood.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} (in more general use) As the preceding, but limited to those that are [[commercial]] [[timber]]s, and are at least [[average]] in hardness.
#: {{ux|en|Ash, hickory and oak are some of the most prominent domestic '''hardwoods'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=10|passage=Mr. Cooke had had a sloop yacht built at Far Harbor, the completion of which had been delayed, and which was but just delivered. […] The Maria had a cabin, which was finished in '''hard wood''' and yellow plush, and accommodations for keeping things cold.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|forestry}} The [[tree]] or tree [[species]] that yields the preceding.
#: {{ux|en|This '''hardwood''' has been planted extensively throughout the hills here.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A joint term for the commercial timbers, without distinguishing which.
#: {{ux|en|You should have used '''hardwood''' for this window sill instead of this junk.}}
# {{lb|en|sports|slang}} The [[sport]] of [[basketball]], in particular, an [[indoor]] basketball [[court]]; so named because the floor of an indoor basketball court is normally made of hardwood.

